{"type":"text","text":"Done. Here's what I found and proved.\n\n## Routine: the area object decoder — entry **$9508**\n\n- **Traced reading PC:** `trace_writes` (mode read) on CPU **$A690**, len 16 (World 1-1's layout records, prgRom **0x2690**) during a level reload returned 496 accesses across frames 117–128. The top PCs were **$9516** (72×, the `CMP #$FD` right after `LDA ($E7),Y` at $9514), $95A1, $95E9, $9522, $9533, $95F3, $962A, $964B, plus $9BC0/$9BC7 (the $9BBB two-byte helper).\n- **Entry:** disassembling back, the instruction is `$9508 LDX #$02` (`STX $08` / `LDA #$00` / `STA $0729` / `LDY $072C` / `LDA ($E7),Y` …). The **loop head is $950A, two bytes later** — exactly the boundary trap the find-routine skill documents.\n- **Callers that settle it:** `JSR $9508` at CPU **$9401** and **$94D3** (prgRom 0x1401/0x14D3), both inside the per-row parser at $93FC, which is dispatched from `JSR $8E04` at $92C8 (table $92CB) from $92B0. No reference was imported.\n\n**What it reads:** through the layout pointer $E7/$E8 (set by LoadAreaData $9C22; 1-1 → $A690) the 2-byte records: byte0 high nibble = X column, low nibble = object type; byte1 bit7 = page-advance flag, bits 4-6 = Y row; `$FD` terminates. It calls $9595 (per-type block/tile decoder) and $9589 (advance record index), looping over the block-buffer slots.\n\n**Proof (ledgered, then undone):**\n- `write_memory` prgRom **0x2691** `0x81 → 0x01` (clearing the page-advance flag of the first 1-1 record), ledger **seq 1**, reason recorded.\n- `reset_emulator`, then Start/Start into World 1-1. The normal first screen (hill, bush, ground, cloud only) instead showed a **? block in the sky, a brick row with two ? blocks, a lone ? block at the far left, and a green pipe at x≈200** — the decoder built the object stream one page earlier.\n- `undo_edit` seq 1 restored 0x81.\n\nRecorded with `record_mapping` (routine, id `0x9508`), promoted, and switched back to hack **default**."}
